--------------------------------------------Linux_Disk_Managing_partition_and_file_system--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Basic Concepts

# Physical / Virtual Disk
        |
        +-- Partition 1
        |      |
        |      +-- Filesystem (ext4)
        |
        +-- Partition 2
        |      |
        |      +-- Filesystem (xfs)
        |
        +-- Partition 3
               |
               +-- Filesystem (swap)


# Disk
A disk is the complete storage device.

Examples:
          /dev/sda
          /dev/sdb


# Filesystem
A filesystem determines how files and directories are stored on a partition.

Common Linux filesystems:

ext4    -> Common Linux filesystem
xfs     -> Enterprise Linux filesystem
btrfs   -> Advanced Linux filesystem
swap    -> Used as virtual memory

Other common filesystems:

ntfs    -> Windows
vfat    -> FAT/EFI partitions

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Disk and Partition Commands

# List disks and partitions
command-> lsblk

Example:
NAME   SIZE TYPE MOUNTPOINTS
sda     50G disk
├─sda1  20G part /
└─sda2  30G part /home
sdb     10G disk
└─sdb1  10G part

# Show filesystem information
command-> lsblk -f

Examples:
NAME   FSTYPE UUID                                 MOUNTPOINTS
sda
├─sda1 ext4   1234-5678                            /
├─sda2 ext4   9876-5432                            /home
sdb
└─sdb1 ext4   abcd-1234

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Check Disk Space
Check filesystem disk usage

command-> df -h
df = Disk Filesystem
-h = Human-readable format

Examples:
Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/sda1       20G   12G  7.0G  64% /

Check directory size:
command-> du -sh /home

Check all directories inside /home:
command-> du -sh /home/*
du = Disk Usage

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. View Partition Information
command-> sudo fdisk -l

# Check a specific disk:
command-> sudo fdisk -l /dev/sdb

Another useful command:
command-> sudo parted -l

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. MBR vs GPT

There are two common partition table types:
MBR

MBR = Master Boot Record

Characteristics:

- Older partitioning system
- Traditionally supports 4 primary partitions
- Maximum disk size around 2 TB

GPT
GPT = GUID Partition Table

Characteristics:

- Modern partitioning system
- Supports many partitions
- Supports very large disks
- Commonly used with UEFI

